1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:03,040
'Ish' is tiny but useful -

2
00:00:03,040 --> 00:00:05,120
especially in informal English.

3
00:00:05,120 --> 00:00:07,200
We add 'ish' to the ends of words

4
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:09,200
to make them more vague,

5
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:11,120
when something is not exact,

6
00:00:11,120 --> 00:00:12,000
for example.

7
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,320
'Ish' makes times more

8
00:00:14,320 --> 00:00:16,640
approximate so '2ish'

9
00:00:16,640 --> 00:00:19,200
is about 2 o'clock.

10
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:21,200
'Okay, I'll see you there at 2ish.'

11
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:23,680
We also add 'ish' to other numbers,

12
00:00:23,680 --> 00:00:25,760
so '40ish' is about 40.

13
00:00:25,760 --> 00:00:27,760
'I'm not sure how old she is -

14
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:29,200
40ish I think.'

15
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:31,680
We add 'ish' to many adjectives

16
00:00:31,680 --> 00:00:33,440
such as colours, so if something

17
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:36,240
is kind of brown, we say it's 'brownish'.

18
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:38,800
'My coat's the brownish one over there.'

19
00:00:38,800 --> 00:00:40,640
And if someone is quite tall,

20
00:00:40,640 --> 00:00:42,480
we can say they are 'tallish'.

21
00:00:42,480 --> 00:00:44,880
'I'm tallish with longish brown hair.'

22
00:00:44,880 --> 00:00:47,200
Finally, because 'ish' has become

23
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:49,520
so popular, it's become its own

24
00:00:49,520 --> 00:00:51,680
word in informal English as a

25
00:00:51,680 --> 00:00:54,000
way to say 'kind of'.

26
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:56,160
'Did you like the film?'

27
00:00:56,160 --> 00:00:57,120
'Yeah, ish.'

28
00:00:57,120 --> 00:00:59,280
I hope you found this video useful -

29
00:00:59,280 --> 00:00:59,780
'ish'.

